  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 PM EDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nTonight-Tuesday...Mostly dry conditions are forecast to continue \nthrough tonight. Some of the hi-res guidance shows perhaps a brief \nshower or two from a sea breeze collision over the interior this \nevening, or developing over the Treasure Coast waters overnight. \nHowever, not much confidence in this, and with the overall dry \nairmass in place will maintain PoPs of 10% or less. Mid to upper \nlevel trough across the eastern U.S. seaboard will begin to pull \nsome moisture northward into the area tomorrow. This will begin a \nreturn of daily showers and storms that will continue through the \nwork week. However, rain chances remain on the lower end for \nTuesday, only around 20-30%, mainly across areas south of Orlando \nand northwest of the I-4 corridor. A weak developing onshore flow \nmay then lead to additional isolated to scattered showers moving \nonshore along the coast into Tuesday night. \n\nLows tonight will not be quite as cool as this morning, but will \nstill drop into the mid to upper 60s in some areas, with other \nlocations not falling below the 70 degree mark. Highs tomorrow will \nremain seasonably warm in the mid to upper 80s, with some added \nhumidity forecast as dewpoints will rise to the upper 60s/low 70s. \n  \nWednesday-Sunday...Trough aloft breaks down across the area, with \nmid level ridge gradually building in from over the western Atlantic \nthis week and across Florida into the weekend. At the surface, high \npressure builds offshore the Mid-Atlantic coast, with ridge axis \nremaining north of the area. This will lead to a developing onshore \nflow that will persist through the weekend. Moisture will continue \nto increase across the area during this time, with PW values rising \nfrom 1.7-2.0\" on Wednesday to 2.0-2.2\" Thursday and Friday. This \nwill continue to increase coverage of showers and storms each day. \nEast-southeast low level winds that will be breezy at times, will \nlead to isolated to scattered onshore moving showers and a few \nstorms during the nighttime and morning hours. This activity will\nthen become scattered to numerous (PoPs ranging from 40-70%) \nalong a diffuse east coast sea breeze from late morning into the \nafternoon hours each day. Warming temps aloft as ridge aloft \ngradually builds in and deeper moisture in place will keep \nstronger storm threat limited. However, can't rule out a few \nstronger storms each day, producing frequent lightning, strong \nwind gusts to 40-50 mph and locally heavy downpours.\n\nHighs will remain near normal for this time of year in the mid to \nupper 80s Wed-Thu, with just a slight warming trend into late week \nand weekend, as highs reach the upper 80s to low 90s as ridge builds \nin. The added humidity is also forecast to lead to a return to \ntriple digit peak heat index values (100-105F) Friday-Sunday. \nStrengthening onshore flow will keep conditions overnight warm \nand muggy, with lows near to above normal in the 70s.",
